@page "/placeholders"
@using Flirty.Placeholders
@inject DesignerPlaceholderSource Source
@inject FlirtyPlaceholderRegistry Registry

<PageTitle>Placeholders · Flirty Designer</PageTitle>

<h1>Placeholders</h1>

<p class="intro">
    Host-declared message placeholders (#140) that this designer knows about. A question text or an
    answer-option label references one with the marker <code>{{key}}</code>; at delivery time the host
    replaces it with a live value.
</p>

@* The delta is stated here as well as beside the input control, because this page is where someone lands
   who wonders why a marker "shows the wrong text" - the honest answer is that the designer never runs the
   host's filler, no matter how complete this list is. *@
<p class="hint">
    <strong>What the designer does not have is the live value.</strong> A filler
    (<code>IPlaceholderFiller</code>) is code and lives in the host process, so a test run here previews the
    declared <strong>sample</strong> value, not the real one — and says so on screen. Declaring a
    placeholder below buys a display name and a sample, not the host's live data.
</p>

<h2>Descriptor file</h2>

<p class="mono">@Source.FilePath</p>

@if (!Source.FileExists)
{
    <p class="empty">
        No descriptor file. That is not an error: without one the designer shows a marker by its raw key,
        and everything else works unchanged.
    </p>
}

@if (Source.Problems.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="banner err">
        <p>
            <strong>@Source.Problems.Count</strong>
            @(Source.Problems.Count == 1 ? "entry was" : "entries were") skipped. The designer keeps
            running, and the remaining entries below were declared.
        </p>
        <ul>
            @foreach (var problem in Source.Problems)
            {
                <li>@problem</li>
            }
        </ul>
    </div>
}

<h2>Declared placeholders</h2>

@if (Registry.Placeholders.Count == 0)
{
    <p class="empty">No placeholder declared.</p>
}
else
{
    <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Marker</th>
                <th>Display name</th>
                <th>Sample value</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var placeholder in Registry.Placeholders)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td class="mono">@($"{{{{{placeholder.Key}}}}}")</td>
                    <td>@placeholder.DisplayName</td>
                    <td class="mono">@(placeholder.Sample ?? "—")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
}

<h2>How to declare one</h2>

<p>
    Create <code>@DesignerApp.PlaceholdersFileName</code> next to
    <code>@DesignerApp.QuestionTypesFileName</code> in the designer's content root and restart. Keys use
    lowercase ASCII letters, digits and <code>-</code>; the sample is any plain text. The file is read once
    at startup and mirrors what the host passes to <code>o.AddPlaceholder(...)</code> — minus the filler,
    which is code and stays in the host.
</p>

<pre class="mono">@ExampleFile</pre>

@code {
    private const string ExampleFile = """
        {
          "placeholders": [
            { "key": "user-name", "displayName": "User name", "sample": "Alice" },
            { "key": "today", "displayName": "Today's date", "sample": "2026-08-07" }
          ]
        }
        """;
}
